despite the fears of those ukrainian  troops the russian foreign minister has  repeatedly said that his country will  not invade sergey lavrov is expected to  meet his u.s counterpart anthony blinken  in coming weeks yes  there won't be a war as far as it  depends on the russian federation we  don't want a war but we won't let our  interests be rudely trampled on and  ignored  the us has rejected all the russian  security proposals for the last two or  three years now they're putting the same  things on the table in other words  they're borrowing from recent russian  initiatives this is something at least  baylor russian and russian militaries  will conduct joint drills next month  russia has been deploying extra soldiers  and fighter jets to its neighbor and  ally in a national address baylor  russian president alexander lukashenko  said that hundreds of thousands of  russian troops were ready to defend the  country  so will there be war or not yes there  will be but only in two cases  if direct aggression is committed  against belarus  if a hot war is unleashed against our  belarus  and the second possibility for war to  happen and for belarus to take part in  is if our ally the russian federation is  directly attacked if the same aggression  is committed against the territory of  the russian federation  well that's the diplomatic front in the  east for the story in the west let's go  live to paris al jazeera's natasha  butler is there uh president macron of  france has spoken to russia's president  what do we know of the call natasha  yes the french and russian presidents  had a phone call we're told by the  elysee that lasted about an hour we  don't have the details yet uh but we are  expecting uh to be briefed by elysee  spokes people a little bit later this  afternoon uh paris time what we know  though is we heard a little earlier from  france's foreign minister who said  really what the situation is is that the  ball he said is in russia's court and  what macron was hoping to get from this  telephone call uh we uh understand the  two main things one of course to try and  diffuse the crisis over ukraine to try  and persuade personally persuade if you  like uh president putin  to follow the path of diplomacy and not  of conflict macro also adamant that he  would give uh putin a very sen putin a  very clear message that of course if  russia was to invade ukraine then  western allies uh would respond uh with  severe sanctions uh the other thing  macro was hoping to do in this telephone  call is trying to understand exactly  what russia's intentions are is it  intending to ukraine russia certainly  invade ukraine russia certainly says it  has no intention to do so but macro  wants to know why is there such a troop  build up uh on the border more  transparency uh from moscow  natasha many thanks al jazeera's natasha  butler they're live in paris let's get a  little geographical context now russia  has troops and military equipment on its  border with ukraine to the north in  belarus where they due to take part in  war games and to the south in crimea  which russia annexed in 2014.  andreas krieg is with us in london he's  an associate professor in the defense  studies department at king's college  london and the author of surrogate  warfare the transformation of war in the  21st century good to have you with us  andreas does the number of troops that  russia has  currently stationed at ukraine's border  indicate to you that an incursion or  invasion is a distinct possibility as  the u.s says  well  it all depends on the political  objectives ultimately that russia has so  any campaign plan operational plan will  stem from the ultimate objective that  russia is trying to pursue is russia  ready to have a war in ukraine of course  it could do that but it comes at an  immense risk and especially in the land  warfare domain you know 100 000 troops  certainly looks a lot it would be a  combined  arms approach but it will come with at a  very high cost and with a lot of risks  also for casualties on both sides  because i think in the land warfare  domain particularly on the eastern and  northern flank of the ukraine the  ukraine is very fairly well defended  especially after training and and  supplies coming in from the west over  the last  seven eight years so it ukraine is a lot  more ready to defend itself now than it  was in 2014 but there is a weak  underbelly i mean the weak underbelly  and i think you've seen that on that map  is is really to towards the south now  with crimea being occupied by russia um  you know there is a possibility of um  you know the the russians using the  their advantage in the naval space or in  the air air power space so the naval  space would mean a merit a a maritime  landing uh and a a an amphibious landing  but this would be very difficult to do  and would also come with its own risk so  russia knows this will be a very high  cost escalation if they do so i also  would say that you know in terms of  logistics the russians are not really  yet ready yet to maintain a a campaign  into deep in ukraine because the  environment isn't as permissive i was  going to say a hundred thousand sounds a  lot but is it enough what is russia's  strategy here is it all about  uh pressuring the west and nato into  making concessions is it a good strategy  is it working or has russia  miscalculated  well i mean it's i'm still up for  speculation i also would say that the  kremlin really hasn't up it made up its  mind of what it is that it can achieve  really um i think belarus is a very  important point that you already um  stated in the initial video because here  we've seen the russians actually getting  into belarus after everything that  happened there over the last two three  years um and doing so without any  condemnation from the west it has  happened quietly because everyone is  focused on ukraine i think there is  already an objective that that russia  has achieved in terms of the negotiation  over whether ukraine would actually  never join nato which is the kind of the  request that the russians are making it  seems like the west is uh is really  diametrically opposed in their  objectives there doesn't seem to be an a  real um compromise at this point in time  that they can walk towards the problem  with the russians is they don't have  they haven't really created an off-ramp  for themselves for them to step away  from uh from this escalation into a  diplomatic realm um so it really depends  if a proper invasion and i mean a  multi-uh like a a combined arms  operation deeply into ukrainian  territory will be very very difficult um  if it's about toppling the regime in  kiev or toppling the political status  for creating chaos that can be achieved  through a variety of different means  which doesn't necessarily need an  invasion of 100 000 troops it can be  done through surrogates it can be done  in the information space through cyber  operations and it can be done by a very  small very  focused operation um in in eastern  ukraine but all of that will come with  huge economic costs for russia and it  will come with reputational costs for  russia as well and the question is is  that something that russia is willing to  bear is that a burden that russia is  willing to bear and i think negotiations  are already going in the right direction  i think what russia wanted to have  ultimately is having a common ground  with uh finding common ground with the  west to negotiate and i think they've  gotten this they're now sitting at the  table they are talking um and i think  that is probably what the russians are  trying to achieve i cannot imagine that  they are interested in a major combat  operation because nothing what russia  has done over the last 30 years would  prepare the military to actually conduct  an operation such as this andreas good  to talk to you many thanks indeed andres  creek there in london

